WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.360
Part 3. You will hear a trainee science teacher called Claire talking about her practical

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:15.080
teaching work to another trainee called Jake. First, you have some time to look at questions

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.240
21 to 25.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:57.480
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 25.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Hi Jake. How are you getting on with the practical teaching?

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
It's harder than I expected, but I've got some great classes. How about you?

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Not brilliant. I'm really struggling with my Year 12 science class.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Are they hard to control?

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Well, I don't have discipline problems as such. It's just that they don't seem to think

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.000
that science has anything to do with their lives. It's depressing. They listen to what

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:33.000
I say, and I gave them a test last week and the results weren't too bad, but there's no

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.000
real engagement.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Right.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.000
And as part of my teaching practice, I have to design an experiment for them to do. I

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.000
was wondering about something on the children's diets, you know, asking them to record what

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.000
they eat and maybe linking it to their state of health.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Hmm, let's think. So your methodology would involve the children recording what they eat,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
OK, but you'd also need to have access to the children's medical records, and I don't

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.000
think people would be happy about that. Confidentiality would be an issue. If you could get the right

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.000
data, the conclusions might be significant, but I suspect it's not going to be easy.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Right.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Have you thought about doing an experiment using animals?

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Wouldn't that be upsetting for the children?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Well, the animals don't have to be harmed in any way. It could just be an experiment where

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.000
they're given a certain diet and the effects are observed.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Would I have to get permission to use animals?

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Yes. You'd have to submit an outline of the experiment and fill in a form, but it's quite

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.000
straightforward.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.000
But if we found out that, say, a particular diet affects the health of animals, the same

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
thing wouldn't necessarily be true for people, would it?

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.000
No, that's true. But the findings for any experiment are going to be limited. It's inevitable.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:04.000
I suppose so. So what animals could I use to investigate the effects of diet? Mice?

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Yes. You'd need experimental mice, ones that have been specially bred for experiments.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:16.000
OK, so what will your experiment be investigating exactly?

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Well, something to do with nutrition. So maybe we could look at food supplements, things

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.000
like extra iron and extra protein and their impact on health.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:34.000
That might be rather broad. Maybe just look at the effects of one supplement, like sugar,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.000
on the health of the mice?

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.000
In fact, maybe the focus could be on whether mice can control their own diet.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.000
So what happens when they have access to more sugar that they don't really need?

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Exactly. Do they eat it, or do they decide to leave it?

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Great. Then later on, you could do a follow-up experiment, adding another variable. Like,

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:02.000
you could give some of the mice the chance to be more active, running on a wheel or something,

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
and the others just sit around and don't do much.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Or I could repeat the experiment but change the type of food I provided, or use mice with

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
a different genetic structure. But I think your idea would be more interesting.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.000
I might think about that some more.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 26 to 30.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:43.000
Now listen, and answer questions 26 to 30.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.000
So, can I talk through a possible procedure for the experiment?

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Yes, you can.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.000
So, I'm going to ask you to answer questions 26 to 30.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.000
OK.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
So, I'm going to ask you to answer questions 26 to 30.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:08.000
So, can I talk through a possible procedure for the experiment where mice are given a sugar supplement?

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Sure. I did a similar experiment in college, actually.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Great. So, how many mice would I need?

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.000
I'd say about 12, and all young ones, not a mixture of old and young.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:28.000
OK. And I'd need two groups of equal sizes, so six in each group.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.000
And how would I tell them apart?

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:37.000
I suppose I could put some sort of tag on one group, or just mark them in some way.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.000
You could use food colouring, that wouldn't hurt them.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Perfect. Then each group would go into a separate cage, and one group,

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
let's call them group A, would be the control group.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.000
So, they'd just have ordinary mouse food. I suppose you can buy that?

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.000
Yes, it comes in dry pellets.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.000
And the other group would have the same as the first group, but they'd also have the extra sugar.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Would you just give them straight sugar?

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:07.000
It might be better to give them something like cereal with it.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Hmm. Then you'd need to weigh the mice, I should think, once a week.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.000
And you'd need an electronic balance.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.000
But we can't hold them on the balance, or it'd affect the reading.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Exactly. So you need something called a weighing chamber to stop the mice from running away.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:31.000
It sounds complicated, but actually you can just use a plastic box with holes in the top.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.000
OK. So once we've measured the weight gain of each mouse,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.000
we can work out the average for each group, as well as the standard deviation,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.000
and then see where we go from there.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:47.000
That sounds cool. I think the students will enjoy it.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Yes. One thing you must remember...

